\par \page }{\b\f1 Introduction
\par }{\f1 
\par Unimodem\'99 the Universal Modem Driver, is included in Windows 95\'99, Windows 98\'99, Windows NT\'99 4.0 and 5.0.  In this document, Unimodem refers to any version.
\par 
\par Today, the overwhelming application for modems is connection to an Internet Service Provider (ISP), e.g.
 AOL, CompuServe, MSN, AT&T, Netcom, etc.  This is such a significant business that ISP connection is overtaking G3 FAX traffic as a fraction of US telephone call connect time.
\par 
\par Many users are having bad experiences with their ISPs.  According to 1997 ISP 
industry surveys, over 10% of calls into ISPs fail (for reasons other than busy), and another 5-10% fail during a call.  In 1998 surveys, these numbers had improved, but there is a long ways to go.  With the move to PCM modems (e.g. X2\'99, K56Plus\'99
, ITU V.90), these problems are likely to get worse in the short term.
\par 
\par The ISP industry (ISPs, telephone companies, modem companies, communications software companies) spends a lot of effort trying to diagnose these problems, and work with
 the local telephone companies or server-side modem vendors when they detect anything they can actually do something about.
\par 
\par All of the members of this industry need to collaborate on means to identify and solve these problems.  Therefore, a critical step 
is to define and use means for the modems to collect and report call diagnostics.  These results can be used in conjunction with other standard commands and responses (e.g. connect result codes, Caller ID reports, etc).  This specification leverages off o
f industry experience, such as the ATI6 and ATI11 commands (3Com).
\par 
\par Note that TIA committee TR-30 and ITU-T Study Group 16 have expressed interest to develop a modem diagnostics specification, possibly descended from this one.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1 Scope
\par }{\f1 
\par This specification def
ines a command for use in collecting modem call diagnostics information at the end of a PSTN data call.  Facsimile, voice and SVD calls may be the added in subsequent drafts.  Higher layer negotiations (e.g. PPP) which are implemented above the modem are 
excluded.
\par 
\par This specification is oriented towards data calls, with a little that is applicable to fax and multimedia calls.  Applicability to voice (e.g. V.253), mobile (e.g. GSM digital cellular) ISDN, ADSL (e.g. T1.413, G.lite) or Cable modems is beyond t
he scope of this version of this specification.
\par 
\par This specification does not address the syntax of results to a DTE test command, e.g. AT#UD=?.
\par It is recommended that modems report ERROR to that command until a specification for that syntax exists.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
