\par 1) Some operating systems cannot deal with multiple report lines in rapid succession.  Modems many insert a delay (e.g. 10ms) between information text lines.
\par 
\par 2) The cod
e tables include values for data and fax calls.  Some of the codes are applicable only to data calls (e.g. data compression), some are applicable only to call origination (e.g. busy, answering signal detection) and some are applicable only to the answerin
g modem (e.g. calling signal detection).
\par 
\par 3) V.58 references
\par }\pard \li720\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 Tables 1-4 are not based on V.58.
\par Table 5 is based on V.34.
\par Tables 6-9 are based on V.58.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par 4) Signal power and delay reports.
\par This specification does not mandate which signal power values to suppo
rt, or the sensitivity of noise detection, or the extent to which the DCE can measure echo delay.  For all of these line parameters, the DCE shall be capable of reporting these values to the extent to which it can detect or control them.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 5) PCM modem parameters
\par This specification is being developed while V.90 is in development in ITU-T SG16.  This specification covers many parameters considered valuable for diagnosing a PCM modem connection, but it is likely that future versions of this specification will 
add parameters based on V.90.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 6) Modulation reports, Table 6
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 Status report Keys 20 and 21 reference Table 6.  Table 6 is derived from V.58.
\par This specification does not mandate which carriers must be supported.  For all carriers that are supported, the DCE shall be capable of reporting that modulation using the appropriate Table 6 code point.
\par 
\par 7) Carrier Frequency has no meaning for PCM modulation.  Values reported may be ignored.
\par 
\par 8) Data transmission reports.
\par A continuous connection may exceed this value 
for characters or frames, given enough time.   If these counters hit maximum value, the DCE manufacturer may roll over to 0; monitoring software in the DTE can be held responsible to monitor this information and determine significant digits beyond 32 bits
.  DCE manufacturers may also maintain longer counters, and report these bits.
\par A 24 bit character counter will overrun on a 56,000 bps data connection in about 40 minutes.
\par A 32 bit character counter will overrun on a 56,000 bps data connection in a bit over a week.
\par 
\par 9) Connection error reports.
\par DCE may limit these error counters to 16 bits.   If limited to 16 bits, these counters should limit to the maximum value.  DCE manufacturers may also maintain longer counters, and report these bits.
\par [If a DTE that ignores flow control to the extent of generating 65,535 data overrun or underrun errors, it certainly needs some kind of maintenance.  A connection with 65,535 frame errors may deserve termination.]
\par 
\par Keys 54 and 55 are intended to report data loss due to character overruns in either direction.  The counter for transmit overrun would be incremented each time the modem receives a character from the DTE while the modem\rquote 
s transmit buffer is full, presumably while the modem had indicated flow off, via XOFF or negating CTS (circuit 106).  The counter for receiver overrun would be incremented each time the modem receives a character from the PSTN while the modem\rquote 
s receive buffer is full, presumably while the modem had received a flow off indication from the DTE, via XOFF or negated RTS/RTR (circuit 133).  Receive overrun could also occur if the DCE-DTE port rate was lower than the incoming data rate.
\par }\pard \widctlpar\pnrnot1\adjustright {\f1 
