\par Some Key 01 reports can be checked by comparison to call progress reports issued at the beginning of the call (e.g. Connect, Busy, No Dial Tone).  Others depend on negotiation results.
\par 
\par The DTE-DCE port is a vulnerable point.  The verify functionality, a test program has to deliberately induce transmit overrun errors and receive overrun errors, and then verify that they were detected by the modem.
\par Recommended methodology is to generate a connection at relatively low speed, transmit characters at a rate higher than the line rate while ignoring circuit 106 and to post circuit 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {II.3.2\tab Negotiation elements
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par A large number of elements concern negotiation results.  The local and the remote DCE should agree on these elements.  If they don\rquote t agree, either the diagnostic reporting is flawed, or at least one of the modems is broke
n!  For example, they should agree on multimedia mode, modulation characteristics, error control and data compression, or data can\rquote t be transferred intact.
\par 
\par Other elements represent measurements.  For example, different modems may come up with different me
asurements of estimated noise power.  The modem under test will report its transmit power, but this will not match the measured receive power at the reference modem.  [The difference is of diagnostic value.]  Therefore, software that tests complain should
 not expect these values to match.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {II.3.3\tab Data transfer elements
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par If the link is working correctly, the characters one modem transmits should closely match the characters the receiving modem gets, and vice versa; otherwise, there is a problem to diagnose. 
 The first test for this is to conduct a data transfer session with minimal impairments, to verify that each modem can count.  The second test is to conduct a data transfer with significant impairments, so that errors are encountered and counted, so that 
reports are generated.  The transmitted frame count may exceed the sum of received good frames plus received bad frames, because frame errors may obscure frame boundries.
\par 
\par Reporting is also collaborative between the two modems.   For example, transmit frame
 errors, key 58, is dependent on the feedback from the receiving modem.  Transmit frame errors should correspond to the number of frames that have to be retransmitted due to these reports.  V.250 +MS command is required on both sides to test key 3E, no mo
dulation in common.  It may also be difficult to force retrains, in order to test Key 3F.
\par 
\par }{\b\f1 II.3.4\tab Call termination code points
\par }{\f1 
\par This testing can be complex, due to the difficulty of generating all the termination causes.
\par For example, if a modem is under tes
t, it is easy to automatically generate local call termination, and straightforward to generate normal remote termination (e.g. Key 5F, Disconnection Frame Received).  However, tooling to deliberately generate some causes would require a specially built a
nd instrumented LAPM implementation which could be programmed to generate conditions like protocol violation (key 5C) or SABME Frame Received (Key 60).
\par 
\par Testing must include the following, because they can be generated with common modems and commands:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 1.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\f1 Keys 1-2
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 2.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\f1 Keys 1F-20
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 3.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\f1 Keys 2C, 2F, 3C
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 4.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\f1 Keys 50-52
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs20\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls18\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls18\adjustright {\f1 Key 5B
