\par \page }{\b\f1 Appendix III, commentary on the value of individual diagnostic elements.
\par }{\f1 
\par All of the elements in this modem diagnostic specification are considered valuable for diagnosing connection problems.  This section is an informative explanation for some of these elements.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {III.1\tab Line conditions
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par The classic tech support explanation of modem problems is \ldblquote line noise\rdblquote .  In some cases, this is still true.  Keys 10-8 are directed at line conditions.  Key 18 is particula
rly valuable for V.34 connections, because it encodes what the modems think about the line.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {III.2\tab Carrier and protocol negotiation
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par The modems are most vulnerable to line conditions, and changing line conditions, in negotiation and training.  Disparities between the two modems are fatal.
\par 
\par This should be straightforward, but there is abundant anecdotal evidence (particularly from ISPs) that there are problems and bugs here.  Some of these have been fixed in the last year or so, and this may be partial cause
 why average connection failure rates have dropped from 16.2% to 8.9% in the last year.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {III.3\tab Carrier and protocol operation
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par Retrains and rate renegotiations are common on V.34 links.  So are unexplained call disconnection events.  Catching these events is a big value for the diagnostics specification.
\par 
\par There is anecdotal evidence of bugs in the LAPM implementations of some modems that cause random errors and disconnection.  Keys 5A-64 are intended to catch these problems.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {III.4\tab Local port problems
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par At 1
15,200 bps, the electrical characteristics of serial ports are stressed.  In EIA/TIA-232-F, circuits with the classic +/-12v swing are not recommended at speeds over 20,000 bps.  Using the lower voltage electrical characteristics in EIA/TIA-562 (+/-5v), t
hese are still not recommended to run over 60,000 bps.  Therefore, serial port data errors are possible.
\par 
\par Also, in a busy computer operating system, loss of data is probable if the UART is unbuffered (16450), because the interrupt response required is under 100 microseconds per character.  The buffers in the common 16550 mitigate this, but it is still a risk.

\par 
\par Also, data can be lost if the internal bus used to connect to the port inside the PC are tied up by other drivers.  For example, PCI video cards are n
otorious for monopolizing the PCI bus for long periods, locking other drivers out from servicing ports (e.g. serial or USB).
\par 
\par Data is lost if the transmitting PC sends data too long after the modem has negated circuit 106 (Clear to Send) or sent an XOFF.  
Data is lost if the receiving modem fills its buffers while the PC has negated circuit 133 (Ready to Receive, same lead as Request to Send) or has sent an XOFF.
\par 
\par Also, if the receiving application gets behind, it may not even be directly aware of lost data
.  A comparison of the received data count in that application (e.g. Microsoft RAS) and the result from Key 53 (received characters sent to DTE) may illuminate problems.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs20\cgrid {III.5\tab Call termination events
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\f1 
\par There are a variety of events that can terminate a cal
l.  Many of them are causes most modems are not equipped to detect.  There is anecdotal evidence that many users do not understand that Call Waiting signals can interrupt their modem connection.  (Note: Windows TAPI has standard provisions for sending Cal
l Waiting disabling DTMF sequences for data calls, but they are often ignored.)  In a home, somebody picking up another extension and trying to dial is another common one.
\par 
\par }}